They are vendored here deliberately and left untouched so that the
simulator shares its wire codec with the LEGO reference implementation.
Any divergence between the simulator and a real hub therefore cannot come
from a difference in framing or serialization -- it can only come from
behaviour, which is what we actually want to test.

Note that messages.py is written from the CLIENT's point of view: requests
know how to serialize, responses know how to deserialize. The simulator is
the HUB, so it needs the mirror image. That lives in spike_sim/wire.py,
and tests/test_wire_conformance.py checks the two halves against each
other.
